WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.496
A major Torrance site is proposed for redevelopment.

2
00:00:04.496 --> 00:00:06.223
Construction unions see jobs.

3
00:00:06.223 --> 00:00:08.129
Nearby businesses see customers.

4
00:00:08.129 --> 00:00:11.701
A neighborhood group sees traffic and displacement pressure.

5
00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:15.036
The city sees infrastructure costs and possible revenue.

6
00:00:15.036 --> 00:00:17.537
A neighboring city expects more commuters.

7
00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:22.539
The project is local on the agenda, but its coalition and consequences are regional.

8
00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:29.029
This is study preparation, not legal advice, economic forecasting, or a conclusion about any pending project.

9
00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:32.185
Coalition analysis describes interests and influence.

10
00:00:32.185 --> 00:00:34.507
It does not establish improper conduct.

11
00:00:34.507 --> 00:00:44.511
For a real Torrance matter, use current public records, verified financial and environmental data, the staff report, regional agency guidance, and city attorney advice.

12
00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:52.589
A growth coalition is an alignment of actors who benefit from development or increasing the economic use and exchange value of place.

13
00:00:52.589 --> 00:01:06.619
Depending on the issue, it may include landowners, developers, lenders, construction interests, businesses, media, institutions, consultants, and public officials seeking jobs, revenue, housing, infrastructure, or civic prominence.

14
00:01:06.619 --> 00:01:10.689
Coalitions are not necessarily formal, stable, secret, or unlawful.

15
00:01:10.689 --> 00:01:14.090
The analytical value lies in tracing aligned incentives.

16
00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:16.459
A landowner wants feasible entitlement.

17
00:01:16.459 --> 00:01:17.917
A contractor wants work.

18
00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:20.832
A city may want tax base or public improvements.

19
00:01:20.832 --> 00:01:22.715
An employer wants labor access.

20
00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:26.359
Their reasons differ, but they may support the same project.

21
00:01:26.359 --> 00:01:35.652
Opposing coalitions may also form among residents, environmental groups, competing businesses, tenant advocates, preservation groups, or fiscal skeptics.

22
00:01:35.652 --> 00:01:38.385
Avoid a simple growth versus no-growth story.

23
00:01:38.385 --> 00:01:48.042
Actors may support housing but oppose warehouse expansion, support infill but resist demolition, or favor economic development while demanding labor standards.

24
00:01:48.042 --> 00:01:53.509
Positions can change when design, financing, public benefits, or market conditions change.

25
00:01:53.509 --> 00:01:56.910
Map interests by issue, not by permanent moral category.

26
00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.372
Distinguish use value from exchange value.

27
00:01:59.372 --> 00:02:05.762
Use value concerns what a place provides to people who live, work, travel, gather, or receive services there.

28
00:02:05.762 --> 00:02:09.103
Exchange value concerns its market and revenue potential.

29
00:02:09.103 --> 00:02:13.910
A neighborhood street has use value as access, safety, identity, and social space.

30
00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:18.717
The same land has exchange value based on development rights and expected returns.

31
00:02:18.717 --> 00:02:26.044
Planning disputes often arise because a proposal increases one value for some actors while reducing another value for others.

32
00:02:26.044 --> 00:02:31.555
Public agencies mediate these conflicts through law, infrastructure, budgets, and negotiation.

33
00:02:31.555 --> 00:02:33.841
They also have institutional interests.

34
00:02:33.841 --> 00:02:41.169
A city may value economic activity because services require revenue, but the gross revenue headline is not the fiscal answer.

35
00:02:41.169 --> 00:02:46.620
New activity can create costs for roads, public safety, parks, administration, and utilities.

36
00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.789
Some revenue is restricted or shared.

37
00:02:48.789 --> 00:02:49.669
Timing matters.

38
00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:53.420
A project can produce short-term fees and long-term obligations.

39
00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.824
Conduct a fiscal analysis with scenarios.

40
00:02:55.824 --> 00:03:03.444
Identify one-time and recurring revenues, one-time and recurring costs, capital replacement, service assumptions, and uncertainty.

41
00:03:03.444 --> 00:03:08.310
Compare the project with a realistic no-project baseline, not with an empty future.

42
00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:14.172
Ask who pays for infrastructure, when payment occurs, and what happens if development phases change.

43
00:03:14.172 --> 00:03:18.131
Regional spillovers are effects crossing jurisdictional boundaries.

44
00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:27.821
Positive spillovers include jobs, housing supply, transit ridership, public amenities, innovation, or environmental improvements benefiting people outside Torrance.

45
00:03:27.821 --> 00:03:36.211
Negative spillovers can include congestion, pollution, flood risk, service burdens, displacement pressure, or infrastructure demand elsewhere.

46
00:03:36.211 --> 00:03:40.052
An effect can be positive for one group and negative for another.

47
00:03:40.052 --> 00:03:44.365
The spillover concept explains why higher levels of government intervene.

48
00:03:44.365 --> 00:03:49.269
If each city captures benefits while exporting costs, regional outcomes can worsen.

49
00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:54.174
If a city bears costs while neighbors receive benefits, underinvestment can result.

50
00:03:54.174 --> 00:04:06.405
Regional planning, funding formulas, environmental regulation, fair-share housing systems, and interagency agreements attempt to address these mismatches, though they create their own distribution questions.

51
00:04:06.405 --> 00:04:08.414
Scale changes the apparent answer.

52
00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:15.268
A project may increase traffic at one intersection yet reduce regional vehicle travel by locating housing near jobs.

53
00:04:15.268 --> 00:04:20.290
It may expand the regional housing supply while creating localized displacement risk.

54
00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:25.371
It may improve city revenue while shifting infrastructure costs to a special district.

55
00:04:25.371 --> 00:04:31.930
Analyze parcel, neighborhood, city, subregion, and metropolitan scales separately before combining conclusions.

56
00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:35.903
Distribution within a jurisdiction matters as much as net totals.

57
00:04:35.903 --> 00:04:40.793
A citywide fiscal gain can coexist with concentrated noise or displacement risk.

58
00:04:40.793 --> 00:04:45.989
Report both the aggregate result and its distribution by place, population, and time.

59
00:04:45.989 --> 00:04:50.634
The Southern California region includes institutions with specialized roles.

60
00:04:50.634 --> 00:04:58.886
SCAG performs metropolitan and regional planning functions, including processes connected to regional transportation and housing needs.

61
00:04:58.886 --> 00:05:04.204
The South Coast Air Quality Management District regulates major aspects of air quality.

62
00:05:04.204 --> 00:05:11.233
Los Angeles County and its agencies provide or coordinate services and infrastructure according to their authority.

63
00:05:11.233 --> 00:05:18.018
Caltrans, Metro, neighboring cities, utilities, and federal agencies may affect funding, operations, or review.

64
00:05:18.018 --> 00:05:24.619
Confirm the exact agency and current authority instead of invoking "regional government" as a single entity.

65
00:05:24.619 --> 00:05:27.492
Coalitions use narratives as well as resources.

66
00:05:27.492 --> 00:05:36.905
"Jobs," "neighborhood character," "housing crisis," "local control," "fiscal responsibility," and "environmental justice" frame which facts seem relevant.

67
00:05:36.905 --> 00:05:41.001
A framing can identify a real value while still omitting tradeoffs.

68
00:05:41.001 --> 00:05:49.925
Ask what the narrative highlights, what it hides, what evidence would test it, and who gains if it becomes the official definition of the problem.

69
00:05:49.925 --> 00:05:52.431
Public benefits deserve special scrutiny.

70
00:05:52.431 --> 00:05:58.421
A project may offer infrastructure, affordable units, open space, community programs, or payments.

71
00:05:58.421 --> 00:06:08.018
Determine whether each item is legally required mitigation, compliance with existing law, a negotiated benefit under valid authority, or a voluntary feature.

72
00:06:08.018 --> 00:06:10.157
Avoid counting the same item twice.

73
00:06:10.157 --> 00:06:17.003
Verify delivery, timing, maintenance, enforcement, and the relationship between the benefit and project impacts.

74
00:06:17.003 --> 00:06:18.754
Apply the method to Torrance.

75
00:06:18.754 --> 00:06:23.705
Imagine redevelopment near major retail, employment, and transportation corridors.

76
00:06:23.705 --> 00:06:26.302
Build a coalition map from public evidence.

77
00:06:26.302 --> 00:06:37.472
Identify property interests, applicant team, financing dependencies, labor and business positions, resident and tenant groups, public agencies, utilities, and neighboring jurisdictions.

78
00:06:37.472 --> 00:06:40.612
Do not infer a hidden agreement from shared support.

79
00:06:40.612 --> 00:06:44.476
Mark documented coordination separately from aligned incentives.

80
00:06:44.476 --> 00:06:46.288
Next build a spillover ledger.

81
00:06:46.288 --> 00:06:50.393
At the site scale, record land use, design, remediation, and access.

82
00:06:50.393 --> 00:06:56.250
At the neighborhood scale, record safety, services, rents, business operations, and construction.

83
00:06:56.250 --> 00:07:02.349
At the city scale, record housing obligations, fiscal effects, infrastructure, and public facilities.

84
00:07:02.349 --> 00:07:09.292
At the regional scale, record jobs-housing relationships, vehicle travel, air quality, transit, and market effects.

85
00:07:09.292 --> 00:07:14.304
For every entry, identify affected groups, evidence, time horizon, and uncertainty.

86
00:07:14.304 --> 00:07:15.693
Then test alternatives.

87
00:07:15.693 --> 00:07:20.765
A lower-density option may reduce a local impact but also reduce housing or revenue.

88
00:07:20.765 --> 00:07:25.656
A higher-density option may support transit but require greater utility capacity.

89
00:07:25.656 --> 00:07:29.158
A mixed-use option may change trip patterns and financing.

90
00:07:29.158 --> 00:07:33.082
A no-project condition may not preserve the current site forever.

91
00:07:33.082 --> 00:07:42.019
Compare feasible alternatives using common measures, and distinguish legal environmental alternatives analysis from broader policy scenario testing.

92
00:07:42.019 --> 00:07:45.098
Regional coordination is practical, not ceremonial.

93
00:07:45.098 --> 00:07:48.781
Submit technically grounded comments to responsible agencies.

94
00:07:48.781 --> 00:07:51.136
Align capital schedules where possible.

95
00:07:51.136 --> 00:07:54.216
Use memoranda or agreements when authority permits.

96
00:07:54.216 --> 00:07:55.665
Seek grant partnerships.

97
00:07:55.665 --> 00:07:57.053
Share data definitions.

98
00:07:57.053 --> 00:08:01.522
Preserve local accountability by stating which city made which commitment.

99
00:08:01.522 --> 00:08:05.084
Cooperation should clarify responsibility, not dissolve it.

100
00:08:05.084 --> 00:08:07.012
Here are three recall questions.

101
00:08:07.012 --> 00:08:07.795
Question one.

102
00:08:07.795 --> 00:08:09.422
What is a growth coalition?

103
00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:09.843
Answer.

104
00:08:09.843 --> 00:08:18.398
It is an alignment of actors whose differing interests converge around increasing development or the economic use and exchange value of place.

105
00:08:18.398 --> 00:08:19.181
Question two.

106
00:08:19.181 --> 00:08:21.530
Why analyze multiple geographic scales?

107
00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:21.952
Answer.

108
00:08:21.952 --> 00:08:31.832
Because benefits and burdens can look different at the parcel, neighborhood, city, and regional levels, and a conclusion at one scale may hide transfers to another.

109
00:08:31.832 --> 00:08:32.736
Question three.

110
00:08:32.736 --> 00:08:36.230
What four categories can a proposed public benefit occupy?

111
00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:36.651
Answer.

112
00:08:36.651 --> 00:08:43.459
Required mitigation, compliance with existing law, a lawfully negotiated benefit, or a voluntary project feature.

113
00:08:43.459 --> 00:08:47.495
Your learner artifact is a two-page coalition and spillover ledger.

114
00:08:47.495 --> 00:08:53.821
Page one maps actors, documented relationships, incentives, resources, narratives, and decision leverage.

115
00:08:53.821 --> 00:09:00.929
Page two lists benefits and burdens by scale, population, time horizon, evidence, uncertainty, and responsible agency.

116
00:09:00.929 --> 00:09:05.026
Add a line for the realistic baseline and each feasible alternative.

117
00:09:05.026 --> 00:09:18.709
This lesson adapts urban-governance concepts from MIT OpenCourseWare's Frameworks of Urban Governance, attributed under Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 for noncommercial, share-alike use.

118
00:09:18.709 --> 00:09:25.648
It independently explains related APA Planning Officials Training and Institute for Local Government themes.

119
00:09:25.648 --> 00:09:36.119
For current applications, consult authoritative SCAG, air-district, county, state, and City of Torrance materials, the current staff report, and the city attorney.
